# {{non-gloss|Expressing distance or motion.}}
## {{lb|en|now|obsolete|or|dialectal|except in phrases}} [[from|From]] (a place); [[off]]. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Take the chicken out '''of''' the freezer.}}
##* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|15.10|2|XIII|x|passage=Sir said Galahad by this shelde ben many merueils fallen / Sir sayd the knyght hit befelle after the passion of our lord Ihesu Crist xxxij yere that Ioseph of Armathye the gentyl knyghte / the whiche took doune oure lord '''of''' the hooly Crosse att that tyme he departed from Iherusalem with a grete party of his kynred with hym|translation=Sir, said Galahad, by this shield many marvels have fallen / Sir, said the knight, it befell after the passion of our lord Jesus Christ, year 32, when Joseph of Arimathea, the gentile knight, / took down our lord '''of''' the holy Cross. At that time, he departed from Jerusalem with a great party of his kindred with him}}
<!--Prior quote:##*:Joseph of Aramathy, that jantyll knyght the whych toke downe Oure Lorde '''of''' the Holy Crosse-->
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|partition=2|member=5|section=3|subsection=2|passage=Against headache, vertigo, vapours which ascend forth '''of''' the stomach to molest the head, read Hercules de Saxonia and others.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|_|except in phrases}} [[since|Since]], [[from]] (a given time, earlier state etc.). {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He hasn't been well [[of late|'''of late''']].}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Mark|IX|passage=And he axed his father: howe longe is it agoo, sens this hath happened hym? And he sayde, '''of''' a chylde.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|act=IV|scene=iv|passage=one that I brought vp '''of''' a puppy {{...}} I was sent to deliuer him, as a present to Mistris Siluia, from my Master.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=29 July 2010|author=Simon Tisdall|passage=Obama has been obliged to make nice '''of''' late in hope of rescuing the moribund two-state process and preventing resumed West Bank settlement building.}}
## [[from|From]], away from (a position, number, distance etc.). {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|There are no shops within twenty miles '''of''' the cottage.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 30 1932|journal=[[w:Time (magazine)|Time]]
|passage=Though Washington does not officially recognize Moscow, the Hoover Administration permits a Soviet Russian Information Bureau to flourish in a modest red brick house on Massachusetts Avenue, within a mile '''of''' the White House.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=7 November 2010|passage=There are now upwards '''of''' 1.4 million 99ers in America facing a life with no benefits and few prospects for finding a job in a market in which companies are still not hiring.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:CalMatters|title=How Airbnb is fighting for its place on California's coast|journal=w:San Francisco Chronicle|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260928044005/https://www.sfchronicle.com/bayarea/article/airbnb-fighting-on-california-s-coast-in-bay-22450830.php|date=27 September 2026
|passage=Situated just south '''of''' San Francisco, Pacifica is home to roughly 37,000 people and is divided along Highway 1 by the coastal zone.}}
# {{non-gloss|Expressing separation.}}
## {{n-g|Indicating [[removal]], [[absence]] or [[separation]], with the action indicated by a [[transitive verb]] and the quality or substance by a grammatical object.}} {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Finally she was relieved '''of''' the burden of caring for her sick husband.}}
##* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|15.18|2|XIII|xviij|passage=And ther with on his handes and on his knees he wente soo nyghe that he touched the holy vessel / and kyste hit / and anone he was hole / and thenne he sayd lord god I thanke the / for I am helyd '''of''' this sekenesse|translation=And there, with his hands and on his knees, he got so close that he touched the holy vessel / and kissed it / and he was whole straight away / and then he said: lord God, I thank thee / for I am healed '''of''' this sickness}}
##* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|book=II|chapter=I|passage=Antigonus [took] upon him to favour a souldier of his by reason of his vertue and valour, to have great care of him, and see whether they could recover him '''of''' a lingering and inward disease which had long tormented him {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 20 1816|author=Jane Austen|journal=Letter
|passage=I am almost entirely cured '''of''' my rheumatism&mdash;just a little pain in my knee now and then, to make me remember what it was, and keep on flannel.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=3 September 1951|journal=[[w:Time (magazine)|Time]]
|passage=In Houston, ten minutes after the Lindquist Finance Corp. was robbed '''of''' $447, Office Manager Howard Willson got a phone call from the thief who complained: "You didn't have enough money over there."}}
## {{n-g|Indicating removal, absence or separation, with resulting state indicated by an [[adjective]].}} {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He seemed devoid '''of''' human feelings.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 28 1731|author=Jonathan Swift|journal=Letter
|passage=But schemes are perfectly accidental: some will appear barren '''of''' hints and matter, but prove to be fruitful {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=31 October 2010|author=Stuart James|passage=Yet for long spells Villa looked laboured and devoid '''of''' ideas.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{n-g|Indicating removal, absence or separation, construed with an [[intransitive verb]].}} {{defdate|14th|19th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1822|author=Jacob Bailey Moore|title=New Hampshire|volume=1|page=5
|passage=He was kindly treated by the people at Saco, and recovered '''of''' his wounds.}}
# {{non-gloss|Expressing origin.}}
## {{n-g|Indicating an [[ancestral]] source or origin of [[descent]].}} {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The word is believed to be '''of''' Japanese origin.}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Acts|II|passage=They wondred all, and marveylled sayinge amonge themselves: Loke, are not all these which speake '''off''' galile? And howe heare we every man his awne tongue wherein we were boren?}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1954|journal=The Rotarian|section=volume 85:6
|passage=My father was born '''of''' a family of weavers in Manchester, England.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|year=2010|title=The Cost of Repair
|passage=Nothing may come '''of''' these ideas, yet their potential should not be dismissed.}}
## {{n-g|Introducing an [[epithet]] that indicates a [[birthplace]], [[residence]], [[dominion]], or other place associated with the individual.}}
##: {{ux|en|[[Jesus]] '''of''' Nazareth (after hometown)}}
##: {{ux|en|[[w:Anselm of Canterbury|Anselm '''of''' Canterbury]] (after diocese)}}
##: {{ux|en|Anselm '''of''' Aosta (after birthplace)}}
##: {{ux|en|Anselm '''of''' Bec (after monastery)}}
##: {{ux|en|[[w:Pedro II of Brazil|Pedro II '''of''' Brazil]] (after dominion)}}
##: {{ux|en|Mrs Miggins '''of''' Gasworks Road, Mudchester (after place of residence)}}
## {{n-g|Indicating a (non-physical) [[source]] of action or emotion; introducing a [[cause]], [[instigation]]}}; [[from]], out of, as an expression of. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The invention was born '''of''' necessity.}}
##* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|12.19|2|X|xix|passage=Faire knyght said Palomydes me semeth we haue assayed eyther other passyng sore / and yf hit may please the / I requyre the '''of''' thy knyghthode telle me thy name / Sir said the knyȝt to Palomydes / that is me loth to doo / for thou hast done me wronge.|translation=Fair knight, said Palamedes, seems to me we have assayed, either other passing sore / and if it may please thee / I request thee, '''of''' thy knighthood, tell me thy name / Sir, said the knight to Palamedes, / I am loath to do that / for thou hast done me wrong.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1803|author=John Smalley|title=Sermons
|passage=Undoubtedly it is to be understood, that inflicting deserved punishment on all evil doers, '''of''' right, belongs to God.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=3 December 2008|author=Rowenna Davis|passage=The woman who danced for me said she was there '''of''' her own free will, but when I pushed a bit further, I discovered that she "owed a man a lot of money", and had to pay it back quickly.}}
## {{lb|en|following an intransitive verb}} {{n-g|Indicates the source or [[cause]] of the verb.}} {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|It is said that she died '''of''' a broken heart.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Joyce Carol Oates|title=The Female of the Species
|passage=He smelled '''of''' beer and cigarette smoke and his own body.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=5 October 2010|author=Rebecca Omonira-Oyekanmi|passage=Two men, one from Somalia and one from Zimbabwe, died '''of''' terminal illnesses shortly after their incarceration ended.}}
## {{lb|en|following an adjective}} {{n-g|Indicates the subject or cause of the adjective.}} {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I am tired '''of''' all this nonsense.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 23 2010|author=Bagehot|journal=The Economist
|passage=Lib Dems were appalled by Mr Boles’s offer, however kindly meant: the party is so frightened '''of''' losing its independence under Mr Clegg that such a pact would “kill” him, says a senior member.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Vincent J. M. DiMaio|title=Gunshot Wounds
|passage=Thus, one finds individuals dead '''of''' a gunshot wound with potentially lethal levels of drugs.}}
# {{non-gloss|Expressing agency.}}
## {{lb|en|following a [[passive]] verb}} {{n-g|Indicates the [[agent]] (for most verbs, now usually expressed with {{m|en|by}}).}} {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I am not particularly enamoured '''of''' this idea.}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Acts|IX|passage=After a good while, the iewes toke cousell amonge themselves to kyll him. But their layinges awayte wer knowen '''of''' Saul.}}
##* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|book=II|chapter=I|passage=[S]he might appeare to be the lively patterne of another Lucrece, yet know I certainly that, both before that time and afterward, she had beene enjoyed '''of''' others upon easier composition.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|publisher=The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints|url=http://lds.org/ldsorg/v/index.jsp?vgnextoid=e1fa5f74db46c010VgnVCM1000004d82620aRCRD&locale=0&sourceId=1aba862384d20110VgnVCM100000176f620a____&hideNav=1etcontentLocale=0|title=The Family: A Proclamation to the World
|passage=The family is ordained '''of''' God.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=March 27 2008|title=Selling rhythm to the world
|passage=Colombia and Venezuela share an elegantly restrained style, with much back-stepping, smaller hand-movements and little use of the elaborate, arm-tangling moves beloved '''of''' Cuban dancers.}}
## {{n-g|Used to introduce the "{{w|subjective genitive}}"; following a noun to form the head of a [[postmodifying]] [[noun phrase]]}} ''(see also 'Possession' senses below)''. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The contract can be terminated at any time with the agreement '''of''' both parties.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=Paul Coates|title=Film at the Intersection of High and Mass Culture|page=136
|passage=In ''Blood and Sand'', meanwhile, Valentino repeatedly solicits the attention '''of''' women who have turned away from him.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=December 28 2009|title=Head to head
|passage=Somehow Croatia has escaped the opprobrium '''of''' the likes of the German Christian Democrats and others that are against any rapid enlargement of the European Union to the include rest of the western Balkans.}}
## {{lb|en|following an adjective}} {{n-g|Used to indicate the agent of something described by the adjective.}} {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|It was very brave '''of''' you to speak out like that.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Emma|passage=When this was over, Mr. Woodhouse gratefully observed,—"It is very kind '''of''' you, Mr. Knightley, to come out at this late hour to call upon us."}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=10 January 2007|author=Dorian Lynskey|passage=Morrissey's spokesperson says he is considering the offer. It would perhaps be rude '''of''' him to decline.}}
# {{non-gloss|Expressing composition, substance.}}
## {{lb|en|after a verb expressing construction, making etc.}} {{n-g|Used to indicate the [[material]] or [[substance]] used.}} {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Many 'corks' are now actually made '''of''' plastic.}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Typee|chapter=XIX|page=164|passage=The mallet is made '''of''' a hard heavy wood resembling ebony, is about twelve inches in length, and perhaps two in breadth, with a rounded handle at one end, and in shape is the exact counterpart of one of our four-sided razor-strops.}}
## {{lb|en|directly following a noun}} {{n-g|Used to indicate the material of the just-mentioned object.}} {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|She wore a dress '''of''' silk.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=23 January 2010|author=Simon Mawer|passage=Perhaps symbolically, Van Doesburg was building a house '''of''' straw: he died within a few months of completion, not in Meudon but in Davos, of a heart attack following a bout of asthma.}}
## {{n-g|Indicating the composition of a given [[collective]] or [[quantitative]] noun.}} {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'll have a dozen '''of''' those apples, please.}} 
##: {{ux|en|What a lot '''of''' nonsense!}}
##* {{RQ:Thackeray Barry Lyndon|passage=His papers at this period contain a mass '''of''' very unedifying and uninteresting documents {{...}}.}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=XXXII|page=153|passage=He is never chased; he would run away with rope-walks '''of''' line.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=31 October 2010|author=Polly Vernon|passage=I'd expected to be confronted by oodles '''of''' barely suppressed tension and leather-clad, pouty-mouthed, large-haired sexiness; the visual shorthand of rock gods in general, and {{w|Jon Bon Jovi}} in particular.}}
## {{n-g|Used to link a given [[class]] of things with a specific example of that class.}} {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Germany's formal name is the Federal Republic '''of''' Germany.}}
##: {{ux|en|Welcome to the historic town '''of''' Harwich.}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe Robinson Crusoe|passage=This having the rainy month '''of''' March and April to water it, sprung up very pleasantly, and yielded a very good crop {{...}}.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=30 October 2010|author=Maya Jaggi|passage=In his studio in the Behlendorf woods, near the Baltic city '''of''' Lübeck, Günter Grass reflects on the outcry over his fictive memoir ''Peeling the Onion''.}}
## {{n-g|Links two nouns in near-[[apposition]], with the first qualifying the second}}; "which is also". {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm not driving this wreck '''of''' a car.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=22 August 2010|author=Sean O'Hagan|passage="I'm having a bitch '''of''' a day," he says, after ordering a restorative pint of Guinness and flopping down in a seat by the front window.}}
# {{non-gloss|Introducing subject matter.}}
## {{n-g|Links an [[intransitive verb]], or a [[transitive verb]] and its subject (especially verbs to do with thinking, feeling, expressing etc.), with its subject-matter}}; [[concerning]], with regard to. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm always thinking '''of''' you.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Emma|passage=[H]e spoke '''of''' his uncle with warm regard, was fond of talking '''of''' him {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Eliot Middlemarch|volume=I|book=I|chapter=III|page=44|passage=You must not judge '''of''' Celia's feeling from mine. I think she likes these small pets.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=19 October 2010|author=Rebecca Seal|passage=while producing ''Cook'', which includes more than 250 seasonal recipes by 80 different chefs, we washed up more than 500 times (oh, how I dreamed '''of''' dishwashers).}}
## {{lb|en|following a noun (now chiefly nouns of knowledge, communication etc.)}} {{n-g|Introduces its subject matter}}; [[about]], [[concerning]]. {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He told us the story '''of''' his journey to India.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 21 2010|journal=The Economist
|passage=Recession and rising unemployment have put paid to most thoughts '''of''' further EU enlargement.}}
## {{lb|en|following an adjective}} {{n-g|Introduces its subject matter.}} {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|This behaviour is typical '''of''' teenagers.}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|passage=The same secludedness and isolation to which the schoolmaster whale betakes himself in his advancing years, is true '''of''' all aged Sperm Whales.}}
# {{non-gloss|Having {{w|partitive}} effect.}}
## {{lb|en|following a number or other [[quantitive]] word}} {{n-g|Introduces the whole for which is indicated only the specified [[part]] or segment}}; "from [[among]]". {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Most '''of''' these apples are rotten.}}
##* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|12.58|2|X|lviij|passage=But as for me said sire Gareth I medle not of their maters therfore there is none '''of''' them that loueth me / And for I vnderstande they be murtherers of good knyghtes I lefte theyre company<!--Prior quote:##*:I meddyll nat of their maters; and therefore there is none that lovyth me '''of''' them.-->|translation=But as for me, said Sir Gareth, I do not meddle their matters, therefore none '''of''' them loves me / And, since I understand they are murderers of good knights, I left their company.}}
##* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=VI|passage=The dead bodies of the Koreish were despoiled and insulted; two '''of''' the most obnoxious prisoners were punished with death {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=10 November 2010|author=Michael Wood|passage=Many '''of''' the civilisational achievements of Mesopotamia are the product of that symbiosis.}}
## {{lb|en|following a noun}} {{n-g|Indicates a given part.}} {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|We want a larger slice '''of''' the cake.}}
## {{lb|en|now|archaic|literary|with preceding [[partitive]] word assumed, or as a [[predicate]] after ''to be''}} [[some|Some]], an [[amount]] of, one of. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|On the whole, they seem to be '''of''' the decent sort.}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Matthew|XXV|passage=And the folysshe sayde to the wyse: geve us '''of''' youre oyle, for oure lampes goo out?}}
##* {{RQ:Keats Lamia|poem=Ode to a Nightingale|stanza=1|page=107|passage=My heart aches, and a drowsy numbness pains / My sense, as though '''of''' hemlock I had drunk, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Cooper Redskins|volume=I|chapter=IV|page=56|passage=Dunning, however, is '''of''' the old school, and does not like new faces; so he will have no Irishman at his door, as is the case with two out of three of the houses at which one calls, now-a-days.}}
## {{n-g|Links to a genitive noun or possessive pronoun, with partitive effect (though now often merged with possessive senses, below; see also [[double possessive]]).}} {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He is a friend '''of''' mine.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=27 August 2010|author=Michael Tomasky|passage=In its flattering way, the press tried to invest this habit '''of''' Bush's with the sense that it was indicative of a particularly sharp wit.}}
# {{senseid|en|possession}}{{non-gloss|Expressing possession.}}
## Belonging to, existing in, or taking place in a given [[location]], [[place]] or [[time]]. ''Compare  "origin" senses, above. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He was perhaps the most famous scientist '''of''' the twentieth century.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1774|author=w:Edward Long|title=The History of Jamaica. Or, General Survey of the Antient and Modern State of that Island|section=volume 2, book 2, chapter 7, {{gbooks|xr0NAAAAQAAJ|5|of}}|passage=The building was erected in two years, at the parochial expence, on the foundation of the former one, which was irreparably damaged by the hurricane '''of''' Auguſt, 1712.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=20 August 2003|author=Julian Borger|passage=Within ten seconds, the citizens '''of''' New York, Cleveland, Detroit and Toronto were being given first-hand experience of what it was like to live in the nineteenth century.}}
## Belonging to (a place) through having [[title]], [[ownership]] or [[control]] over it. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The owner '''of''' the nightclub was arrested.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=28 October 1977|passage=In a much-anticipated radio broadcast the Duke '''of''' Edinburgh said last night that Britain will be a grim place in the year 2000 {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|title=Dictionary of National Biography|page=27|passage=The third son, William John (1826-1902), was headmaster '''of''' the Boys' British School, Hitchin {{...}}}}
## Belonging to (someone or something) as something they [[possess]] or have as a characteristic; {{n-g|the "possessive genitive". (With abstract nouns, this intersects with the subjective genitive, above under "agency" senses.)}} {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Keep the handle '''of''' the saucepan away from the flames.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1933|author=Havelock Ellis|title=Psychology of Sex|volume=4|passage=The breasts '''of''' young girls sometimes become tender at puberty in sympathy with the evolution of the sexual organs {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=29 October 2010|author=Marina Hyde|passage=It amounts to knocking on the door '''of''' No 10 then running away.}}
# {{non-gloss|Forming the "objective genitive".}}
## {{n-g|Follows an [[agent noun]], verbal noun or noun of action.}} {{defdate|from 12th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|She had a profound distrust '''of''' the police.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Matthew|4|passage=And he saith unto them, Follow me, and I will make you fishers '''of''' men.}}
# {{non-gloss|Expressing qualities or characteristics.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|or|literary}} {{n-g|Links an adjective with a noun or noun phrase to form a quasi-adverbial [[qualifier]]}}; [[in respect to]], [[as regards]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|My companion seemed affable and easy '''of''' manner.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1879|author=Thomas Raynesford Lounsbury|title=History of the English Language|page=112
|passage=This fact becomes very noticeable when a cultivated tongue ceases to be used any longer by the educated, and is heard only from the mouths of the illiterate. The variations which spring up under such circumstances are easy '''of''' observation, because we have an ideal standard preserved by which to compare the present with the past.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=Zane Gray|title=Wildfire|page=35
|passage=He was huge, raw-boned, knotty, long '''of''' body and long '''of''' leg, with the head of a war charger.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=11 August 2004|author=Sean Ingle|passage=Still nimble '''of''' mind and fleet '''of''' foot, Morris buzzed here and there, linking well and getting stuck in at every opportunity.}}
## {{n-g|Indicates a [[quality]] or [[characteristic]]}}; "characterized by". {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Pooh was said to be a bear '''of''' very little brain.}}
## {{n-g|Indicates [[quantity]], [[age]], [[price]], etc.}} {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|A young man '''of''' 20 has been paying interest at an exorbitant rate '''of''' 10%.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=Frank Norris|title=The Pit|page=4|publisher=Doubleday|year_published=1924|passage=She was a tall young girl '''of''' about twenty-two or three, holding herself erect and with fine dignity.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|year_published=2006|author=Raymond A. Serway; John W. Jewett|title=Principles of Physics|page=428
|passage=A police car, traveling southbound at a speed '''of''' 40.0 m/s, approaches with its siren producing sound at a frequency '''of''' 2 500 Hz.}}
## {{lb|en|US|informal|considered incorrect by some}} {{n-g|Used to link singular indefinite nouns (preceded by the indefinite article) and attributive adjectives modified by certain common adverbs of degree.}}
##: {{ux|en|It's not that big '''of''' a deal. (Standard form: It's not that big a deal.)}}
# {{non-gloss|Expressing a point in time.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|regional}} [[during|During]] the course of (a set period of time, day of the week etc.), now specifically with implied repetition or regularity. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Of''' an evening, we would often go for a stroll along the river.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Works|volume=XV|chapter=VI|chaptername=Picking Up Miss Kimmeens|page=242|passage=For, sometimes '''of''' an afternoon when Miss Pupford has been reading the paper through her little gold eye-glass{{nb...}}, she has become agitated, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:James Maisie|passage=If there was a type Ida despised, Sir Claude communicated to Maisie, it was the man who pottered about town '''of''' a Sunday {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|UK|dialectal|chiefly in the negative}} [[for|For]] (a given length of time). {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I’ve not taken her out '''of''' a goodly long while.}}
## {{lb|en|after a noun}} {{n-g|Indicates [[duration]] of a state, activity etc.}} {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|After a delay '''of''' three hours, the plane finally took off.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|US}} [[before|Before]] (the hour); [[to]] (the hour). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I’ll be ready at ten '''of''' two|t=I’ll be ready at 1:50}}
##: {{ux|en|What's the time? / Nearly a quarter '''of''' three.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'll be ready by five '''of''' noon.}}
##* {{RQ:Time|date=17 June 1940|title=Little Bull Booed|passage="Fellow Democrats," he began, "I left Washington at a quarter '''of''' two this morning {{...}}"}}
### {{lb|en|informal}} Often used without the hour.
###: {{ux|en|I’ll be ready at ten '''of'''|t=I’ll be ready at 1:50, or 2:50, or whatever time ending in 50 makes most sense in context.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|belonging to or associated with}} When applied to a person or persons, the possessive is generally used instead.
* {{sense|containing, comprising{{,}} or made from}} '''''Of''''' may be used directly with a verb or adjectival phrase.
* When modifying a noun, modern English increasingly uses [[noun adjunct]]s rather than '''''of'''''. Examples include ''[[part of speech]]'' (16th century) vs. ''[[word class]]'' (20th century), ''{{w|Federal Bureau of Investigation}}'' (1908) vs. ''{{w|Central Intelligence Agency}}'' (1947), and ''affairs of the world'' (18th century) vs. ''world affairs'' (20th century).
* The use of {{m|en|of}} to link nouns to attributive adjectives modified by certain adverbs (for example "It was too short of a walk" or “It’s not that big of a deal”) is always optional. Omitting {{m|en|of}} in such instances is always permissible and does not alter the meaning of the expression. Adverbs that may be used with this construction include {{m|en|too}}, {{m|en|so}}, {{m|en|how}}, {{m|en|as}}, {{m|en|more}}, {{m|en|less}}, {{m|en|this}}, and {{m|en|that}}.

